  Cell["4. The network", "Section"], 
  Cell[TextData[{"The message on edge p is a force vector ", 
     Cell[BoxData[SubscriptBox["m", "p"]]], " = \[CurlyPhi](\[Rho](", 
     Cell[BoxData[SubscriptBox["r", "p"]]], ")) c(", 
     Cell[BoxData[SubscriptBox["r", "p"]]], ") ", 
     Cell[BoxData[SubscriptBox["u", "p"]]], ", where \[CurlyPhi] is a small \
multilayer perceptron (16 \[RightArrow] 32 \[RightArrow] 32 \[RightArrow] 1, \
tanh). There is one \[CurlyPhi] for bonds and one for non-bonded edges. The \
force on each bead is F = A.m."}], "Text"], 
  Cell[TextData[{"Three properties follow from the construction, not from \
training. (1) Invariance and equivariance: \[CurlyPhi] sees only distances, \
so rotating the chain rotates the forces. (2) Newton's third law: each column \
of A sums to zero, so the total force is exactly zero. (3) Conservative \
forces: a central pair force f(r) u is minus the gradient of U(r) = ", 
     Cell[BoxData[SubsuperscriptBox["\[Integral]", "r", "\[Infinity]"]]], 
     " f(s) ds, so energy can be conserved in MD."}], "Text"], 
  Cell[TextData[{"A repulsive core U0 = (0.8/", 
     Cell[BoxData[SuperscriptBox["r)", "12"]]], " is added to every edge as a \
fixed prior, and \[CurlyPhi] learns only the remainder ", 
     Cell[BoxData[SubscriptBox["F", "ref"]]], " - ", 
     Cell[BoxData[SubscriptBox["F", "prior"]]], ". The networks also see a \
clamped distance: never below 0.88 (closest approach in the data is about \
0.93) and, for bonds, never above 1.45 (the longest bond seen is about 1.44). \
Without these two guards, a long run eventually visits distances the network \
never saw, the extrapolated forces are wrong, and the chain either collapses \
onto itself or breaks a bond. This first happened at N = 32 and N = 64 during \
development."}], "Text"], Cell["net0 = BuildForceNet[topo]", "Input", 
   CellLabel -> "In[7]:="], 
