  Cell["Table[k -> LinearModelFit[Table[{Log[n], Log[stats[n, k][[1]]]}, {n, \
{16, 32, 64}}], s, s][\"BestFitParameters\"][[2]], {k, {\"ref\", \"nn\"}}]", 
   "Input", CellLabel -> "In[18]:="], 
  Cell[BoxData[RowBox[{"{", RowBox[{RowBox[{"\"ref\"", "\[Rule]", 
          "0.7827666331212463`"}], ",", RowBox[{"\"nn\"", "\[Rule]", 
          "0.7544058352616105`"}]}], "}"}]], "Output", 
   CellLabel -> "Out[18]="], 
  Cell[TextData[{"The slope of log ", Cell[BoxData[SubscriptBox["R", "g"]]], 
     " against log N is the effective exponent \[Nu]. For long self-avoiding \
chains it tends to 0.588. These chains are short, start straight, and the N = \
64 runs are short compared with their relaxation time, so both fits come out \
higher (about 0.72). What matters here is that the learned and reference \
forces give the same ", Cell[BoxData[SubscriptBox["R", "g"]]], 
     " at each N, within the error bars."}], "Text"], 
  Cell["8. Limits", "Section"], 
  Cell[TextData[{"\[Bullet] The networks are pair networks: ", 
     Cell[BoxData[SubscriptBox["m", "p"]]], " depends only on ", 
     Cell[BoxData[SubscriptBox["r", "p"]]], ". That is exact for this \
reference model (bonds + WCA), and it is also the theorem in notebook 01: any \
invariant network whose messages depend only on one edge gives a sum of pair \
forces. Bond-angle or torsion potentials would need three- and four-body \
messages (as in NequIP or MACE).\n\[Bullet] The networks are trusted only on \
the distances seen in the data; the core prior and the clamps handle the \
rest. Retrain with a hotter or denser data set to widen that \
range.\n\[Bullet] Everything runs on CPU. A 32-bead model trains in about 3 \
minutes; a Langevin step of the 64-bead chain with the tabulated field takes \
about 25 \[Micro]s with the C compiler."}], "Text"]}, 
 StyleDefinitions -> "Default.nb"]
